Z||000000||FieroSEt||02-16-2001||08:06 AM||||I'm putting a T3(ar.63) into my 86SE 2.8. Does anyone know where all of these lines connect on my 2.8 motor?? what about the EGR tube that is coming off the turbo??  I have looked at Dennis Laguna (sp) website and can't find out anything. I have also emailed him twice. I need some help with this, because  my custon crossover pipe will be ready tommorrow!!  Thank you for any help that I can get!!!  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||c m west||02-16-2001||11:37 AM||||try emailing Chris West here at West Coast Fiero, he is a turbo freak and most likely have the answers to your questions<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000002||squid||02-16-2001||01:46 PM||||It looks like a fiero EGR tube but its the oil return line to the pan. Any exhaust driven turbo will have an oil pressure line, a return line, compressor intake(from the air filter), and compressor output(to the intake/throttle body), and a small vacuum "T" on the compressor section plumbed to the wastegate. Some have water inlets and outlets but I dont think T3's were watercooled. If the turbine wheel/shaft is okay you can rebuild one for @$120 if you can find a bushing kit(yes I said bushing, not bearing). The pieces are balanced individually and the shaft isnt keyed.Some shops will balance the hole thing for you as an assembly. The torque holding it together isnt much either. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSEt||02-16-2001||10:24 PM||||Thank's for the replies. I'll try to e~mail chris west. yes , I was talking about that EGR tube looking deal. thank's for clearing that up for me.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroSEt||02-17-2001||12:35 AM||||<BR>From:Villa Rica, GA USA<BR>Registered: Aug 99 <BR>  posted 02-16-2001 01:46 PM                 <BR>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<BR>It looks like a fiero EGR tube but its the oil return line to the pan. Any exhaust driven turbo will have an oil pressure line, a return line, compressor intake(from the air filter), and compressor output(to the intake/throttle body), and a small vacuum "T" on the compressor section plumbed to the wastegate. Some have water inlets and outlets but I dont think T3's were watercooled. <P>I do have the a oil pressure line(rather large?) about the same size as the EGR t ube?<BR>I do have the vacuum *T* that youre talking about, then the line T's to a selinoid then another line leaves the selinoid and goes to the compressor intake!! Then there's a line that goes from the the intake part off of a *T* there to the compressure output and a second line (metal) that goes about 10 inches then has a rubber hose that is attached. On that *T* on the compressor output there is a second short metal line about 3 inches long that is also connected to a rubber hose that goes somewhere also??  WHERE DOES THESE GO???  anyone have a clue?? are you as confused as I am??   thank's!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||squid||02-17-2001||09:19 PM||||The 10"& 3" metal lines are for water cooling. You could plumb them in-line with your heater core with "T" fittings in your heater hoses. The solenoid controls the amount of vacuum to the wastegate depending on the fuel quality and isnt necessary unless you want to tune for 93 octane and want to install a switch to ingage the solenoid for low octane fuel without detonation. You can always add it later. You should buy a book called "Turbochargers by Hugh MacInnes" published by HPBooks.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroSEt||02-17-2001||11:10 PM||||--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<BR>The 10"& 3" metal lines are for water cooling. You could plumb them in-line with your heater core with "T" fittings in your heater hoses. <P>Thank's squid.... So, IF I decide not to use the water cooler can I just bypass them somehow??? Maybe just plug them??? OR does the turbo HAVE TO HAVE the water cooler hoses hooked up tp operate properly??<BR>||||reg||1
